From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:22:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 31/44] Camera: the wielded item and the empty hand as
 Minecraft draws them

Luanti held the item at a fixed rest pose (made a setting by the
handheld's wield pose patch) and swung it 50 units left, up and down
while turning it towards a fixed pose, pushed 12.5 units away for the
whole swing: at the handheld's 640x480 a sword went round in a circle
from the bottom right up past the crosshair. After a punch the item also
slid left and turned 70 degrees while the punch reloaded.

The wielded item now goes through the transforms of Minecraft's
ItemInHandRenderer (Java 1.9 to today) and of its item models'
firstperson_righthand display, written in Minecraft's order and frame
(right-handed, looking down -z) and flipped into Irrlicht's at the end:
the arm at (0.56, -0.52, -0.72) blocks, the swing's shift by
sin(sqrt(p) pi), sin(2 sqrt(p) pi) and sin(p pi) and its turns of
45 - 20 sin(p^2 pi), -20 sin(sqrt(p) pi) and -80 sin(sqrt(p) pi) degrees
(p is the swing's progress, 0.3 s), then translation (1.13, 3.2, 1.13) / 16
and rotation (0, -90, 25) for an item drawn as an image, or rotation
(0, 45, 0) for a node. The meshes keep their shape: Minecraft's generated
item is the same one-block square as the extruded mesh, 40 units, so a
block is 40 / 0.68 units where Minecraft scales the item by 0.68, and 75
units for a node mesh (30 units at Minecraft's 0.4). An item is a pixel
thick in Minecraft (1/16 of its size) and a tenth here, so its depth is
scaled by 0.625.

An empty slot shows Minecraft's arm: a box of 4x12x4 pixels with the
right arm of the player's skin (textures[0] of the player's model, 64x32
or 64x64, at 40,16), placed by renderPlayerArm with its own swing,
whatever the hand item is (the creative hand too).

The wield camera has Minecraft's 70 degree FOV for the hand instead of a
fixed 72, widened below 16:9 to see as wide as a 16:9 screen; without
that, at 4:3 the item's centre is past the right edge. The off-hand poses
were set under 72 degrees, so the off-hand item comes nearer by the ratio
of the two and stays the same on screen.

While digging, a swing past its half snaps back in 0.05 s and starts
again, as Minecraft repeats the first half when mining, so a dig hits
every 0.2 s and its sound with it; a click still swings once. After a
punch the arm drops by 0.6 block and rises with the cube of the reload
ratio, at most 8 times its range a second (0.4 a tick in Minecraft).

Place swung the hand at every press and repeat on a node, even a sword
on stone, so with a shield held up by the place button, walking up to a
block made the sword swing. As in Minecraft it now swings only when
something can happen: an item that is neither a tool nor the hand (a
node placed, a bucket used), or a node that opens or reacts (rightclickable
or with a formspec) while not sneaking.

The settings wield_position, wield_rotation, wield_node_position and
wield_node_rotation are gone.

Checked in the native build at 640x480: frames at fixed swing progress
and reload ratio for a sword, a pick, a dirt block, an apple, a torch and
the arm, with the outline Minecraft's own math gives drawn over the
sword, the block and the arm (they match); the off-hand shield lowered
and raised, the same as before; a log of the swing while digging stone
(cycles of 0.25 s at 28 FPS); place held on stone with a sword or the
hand (no swing) and with dirt (swings).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

